\1\n    <div id="site-header"></div>
    <!-- Header -->
    
    <main class="lesson-container py-4">
        <!-- Breadcrumb -->
        <div id="tutorial-header"></div>
        
        <div class="content lesson-content" style="font-size: 1.05rem; line-height: 1.7; color: var(--text-secondary);">

            <p><strong>Packet loss</strong> occurs when one or more data packets traveling across a network fail to reach their destination.</p>
            
            <h3>Potential Causes</h3>
            <p>Packet loss is a symptom, not a root cause. Common culprits include:</p>
            <ul>
                <li>Network congestion or overloaded device queues.</li>
                <li>Bad physical cabling or faulty interfaces.</li>
                <li>Wireless interference or weak signal.</li>
                <li>Duplex or speed mismatches.</li>
                <li>ISP or upstream provider issues.</li>
                <li>Traffic policing or shaping policies dropping excess traffic.</li>
                <li>Packets dropped by firewall or security policies.</li>
                <li><a href="mtu-and-mss-explained.html">MTU</a> mismatch leading to unfragmentable packet drops.</li>
            </ul>
            
            <h3>Troubleshooting Workflow</h3>
            <ol>
                <li><strong>Determine Scope:</strong> Is it affecting one device, an entire VLAN, or all Internet-bound traffic?</li>
                <li><strong>Test Local Gateway:</strong> Ping your default gateway to rule out local Wi-Fi/LAN issues. Compare wired vs. wireless behavior.</li>
                <li><strong>Inspect Interfaces:</strong> Log into switches/routers and check interface counters for CRC errors, collisions, or input/output drops.</li>
                <li><strong>Check Utilization:</strong> Check <a href="network-monitoring-fundamentals.html">monitoring systems</a> to see if a link is saturated (100% bandwidth usage).</li>
                <li><strong>Use Path Tools:</strong> Use <code>tracert</code> / <code>traceroute</code> or <code>pathping</code> to identify where loss begins.</li>
            </ol>
            
            <h3>Critical Concept: Interpreting ICMP Loss</h3>
            <p><strong>ICMP loss does not always equal application loss.</strong> Many routers intentionally rate-limit or deprioritize ICMP echo replies generated by their control plane to protect their CPUs. Do not interpret one intermediate-hop loss value (e.g., hop 4 shows 50% loss, but hop 5 and the final destination show 0% loss) as definitive end-to-end packet loss. If the final destination is not experiencing loss, the intermediate hop is merely ignoring the pings.</p>
        
        </div>
        
